<img src="docs/images/pipeline_diagram.png" width="700" alt="5-stage pipeline block diagram">

> [!NOTE]
> 32 instructions are implemented in RTL; 16 are currently verified by the automated testbench. See [Instructions Supported](#instructions-supported) for the exact breakdown.

## Features

- **32 instructions implemented**, covering the full RV32I base ISA: all R-type ALU ops, I-type arithmetic/logic/shift ops, loads/stores (`lw`/`sw`), all six branch comparisons, both jump forms (`jal`/`jalr`), and upper-immediate ops (`lui`/`auipc`)
- **Custom `BITREV` instruction** — a hand-added non-standard opcode extending the base ISA
- **Full hazard handling**: EX/MEM and MEM/WB forwarding, load-use stall detection, control hazard flushing on taken branches
- **Register file write-first bypass** — fixes a same-cycle read/write hazard that standard EX-stage forwarding alone cannot catch (see `docs/bugfix-notes.md` or commit history)
- **Self-checking testbench** with a scoreboard that validates every write-back commit and branch-skip snapshot in program order, cycle-accurately, against expected values
- **Python toolchain**: a mini-assembler (`asm.py`) and reference simulator (`refsim.py`) to generate test programs and golden expected outputs without hand-editing the testbench

## Repository Structure

```
RTL/
├── Pipeline_Top.v         # Top-level pipeline integration
├── Fetch_cycle.v          # IF stage
├── Decode_cycle.v         # ID stage — register read, control/immediate decode
├── Execute_cycle.v        # EX stage — ALU, forwarding muxes
├── Memory_cycle.v         # MEM stage — data memory access
├── writeback_cycle.v      # WB stage — result mux back to register file
├── ALU.v                  # Arithmetic/logic unit
├── ALU_Decoder.v          # funct3/funct7 → ALUControl decode
├── Control_Unit_Top.v     # Top-level control signal generation
├── Main_Decoder.v         # Opcode → control signal decode
├── Hazard_unit.v          # Forwarding, stall, and flush logic
├── Register_File.v        # 32x32 register file (write-first bypass)
├── Sign_Extend.v          # Immediate sign extension
├── PC.v / PC_Adder.v      # Program counter and PC+4/branch target logic
├── Mux.v                  # Generic parameterized mux
├── Instruction_Memory.v   # Instruction ROM
├── Data_Memory.v          # Data RAM
└── pipeline_tb.v          # Self-checking testbench (scoreboard-based)

tools/
├── asm.py                 # Mini-assembler: assembly -> program.hex
└── refsim.py              # Reference ISA simulator: generates expected register values
```

## Instructions Supported

**32 instructions implemented in RTL**, spanning the full RV32I base ISA plus one custom extension:

| Type | Instructions | Count |
|---|---|---|
| R-type ALU | `add, sub, and, or, xor, slt, sltu, sll, srl, sra` | 10 |
| I-type ALU | `addi, slti, sltiu, xori, ori, andi, slli, srli, srai` | 9 |
| Load | `lw` | 1 |
| Store | `sw` | 1 |
| Branch | `beq, bne, blt, bge, bltu, bgeu` | 6 |
| Jump | `jal, jalr` | 2 |
| Upper immediate | `lui, auipc` | 2 |
| Custom | `bitrev` (opcode `0001011`) | 1 |

Note: the ALU decoder doesn't distinguish R-type (`0110011`) from I-type (`0010011`) opcodes except where ADD/SUB share an encoding, so all I-type immediate arithmetic ops share the same decode path as their R-type counterparts and come "for free" once the R-type ALU logic is correct.

### Testbench coverage

The current self-checking testbench (`pipeline_tb.v`) exercises a 16-instruction subset: all 10 R-type ALU ops, `addi`, `lui`, `auipc`, `beq`, `bne`, and `jalr`.

> [!WARNING]
> **Not yet covered by the testbench**: `lw`, `sw`, `jal`, `blt`, `bge`, `bltu`, `bgeu`, the I-type shift/arithmetic ops (`slli, srli, srai, slti, sltiu, xori, ori, andi`), and `bitrev`. These are implemented in RTL but unverified by the automated scoreboard — next priority for testbench expansion.

## Running the Simulation

1. Open the project in Vivado, add all files under `RTL/`.
2. Set `pipeline_tb.v` as the simulation top module.
3. Run behavioral simulation for `1000ns`.
4. Check the TCL console for the `SELF CHECK` / `SUMMARY` output — the testbench reports `PASS`/`FAIL` per instruction and a final error count.

To regenerate the test program:
```bash
python tools/asm.py test_program.asm -o RTL/program.hex
python tools/refsim.py test_program.asm    # prints expected register values for the scoreboard
```

## Verification Approach

The testbench (`pipeline_tb.v`) uses a 26-entry scoreboard that watches every register write-back commit in program order and grades it against the expected value from the reference simulator, rather than only checking final register state. This caught a register-file same-cycle read/write hazard bug that only manifested for instructions exactly 3 apart from their producer in the pipeline — invisible to simpler end-of-simulation-only checks.

## Status

All 16 instructions currently exercised by the self-checking testbench pass with 0 errors. The remaining 16 implemented instructions (loads/stores, `jal`, 4 branch comparisons, I-type shift/logic ops, `bitrev`) are functionally complete in RTL but not yet covered by the automated scoreboard — testbench expansion in progress.
